  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "A 42-cancer single-cell screen releases a tumour microenvironment atlas and scTME software, alongside new tools for immune trafficking and BCR clonotype analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the public release of single-cell resources paired with analytical tools for tumour and immune cell states. The pan-cancer screen is the most direct example: it reports tumour microenvironment programs conserved across 42 cancers, across organs and species, and releases an atlas together with scTME software. X-BCRdb extends this resource layer to B-lineage cells, pairing BCR clonotypes with transcriptomes in a cross-disease single-cell database. CellVADIS addresses a different analytical problem, inferring cross-tissue immune cell trafficking from single-cell snapshots with an optimal transport framework benchmarked on synthetic data. The genomic burden study is related but less directly tied to resource building; it examines how genetic alterations and reversible regulatory programs jointly shape tumour states across cancers. What is new here is the pairing of cross-species conservation analysis with inference of immune dynamics from static snapshots.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
